  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 137 PM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nToday will be mostly sunny and quiet under high pressure with light \nwinds and highs in the upper 70s to low 80s. Northwest flow aloft \nwill bring smoke into the region from wildfires out west. The smoke \nis expected to remain mostly aloft and currently throughout our CWA \nthe air quality is good. For more on air quality and to get an air \nquality forecast for your location please see AirNow.gov\n\nPatchy valley fog is possible early Thursday morning as winds\nbecome light to calm. Fog is most likely east of I-29 over\nnorthwest Iowa. Any fog that forms should burn off by mid-\nmorning. As the high pressure moves off to the east this \nafternoon and evening winds will become southerly. At the same \ntime from the west an upper shortwave will approach the region, \nstrengthening the LLJ. Overnight winds will begin to increase \nfrom the west to the east. During the day Thursday good mixing \nup through the 850 mb level will bring down stronger gusts of 20\nto 30 mph for much of the region. Portions of northwest Iowa \nwill remain lighter at 15 to 20 mph gusts. Conditions are \nexpected to be dry and warmer thanks to modest WAA. Highs will \nrange from the low to upper 80s with warmest conditions over \ncentral South Dakota. \n\nThursday night another subtle upper wave will pivot over the region \nbringing chances for showers and thunderstorms. There are a few \ntrends we will need to monitor over the next 24 to 36 hours. A \nnarrow band of modest moisture return looks to set up over \ncentral South Dakota, but areas to the east look to remain \nfairly dry. Moderate surface based instability of around 1000 \nJ/kg of CAPE will build through Thursday afternoon over central \nSouth Dakota. However, as you move east there is an increasingly\nstrong cap in place. Instability above the cap is lacking, so \nelevated convection would struggle to initiate. So while an \nisolated strong to severe storm is possible over central South \nDakota, confidence in any strong storms making it farther east \nthan US Highway 281 is low. If storms should form it would \nbetween roughly 6 pm and midnight. \n\nFriday sees the return of higher dew points in the upper 60s to low \n70s. With highs in the 70s to 80s it will be a bit of a muggy day. A \nseries of subtle waves will keep low PoPs in the forecast for much \nof the day. A more defined wave pivots through Friday evening into \nSaturday morning bringing chances for more organized convection, \nmainly east of I-29. Periodic storm chances continue through the \nrest of the weekend and into the beginning of next week. However, \nconfidence in timing, coverage, and severity of storms is low as \nthis is the point where mid-range guidance begins to diverge.",
